Step C5 complete.

Built create_watch.py (ExternalCreationWatch, wait_until_settled) and recent_writes.py (RecentWrites) — the external-creation watch from Decision #19, plus the coordination piece it needed.

The real work here was a design gap I caught before writing any code, thinking through the plan rather than discovering it via a failing test: running C4's write handler and C5's raw filesystem watch concurrently on the same directory means C4's own command-triggered creations would also trip C5's watch — a second, spurious, uncorrelated FileCreateCompleted for every ordinary write. Fixed with RecentWrites, a short-TTL (5s) shared record that WriteCommandHandler marks into and ExternalCreationWatch checks against — long enough to bridge watchfiles' own ~1.6s debounce window, not a general write-history log.

Then verified two things empirically that the plan had flagged as open risks rather than assumed:

Settle behavior — watchfiles' "added" event fires on file creation, not when a slow write finishes (confirmed via awatch's own signature: 1600ms default debounce, no "settled" guarantee documented). A 3-chunk slow-write test proves wait_until_settled waits for the real end, asserting on elapsed time so a lucky-but-wrong early return can't pass.
The suppression actually works — the test that matters most here publishes a real FileWriteRequested, confirms the correlated completion arrives, then does a second fetch timed to outlast the full debounce+settle window and asserts it comes back empty — directly reusing the BusClient.fetch() idle-returns-[] fix from C4.
