Track C complete — Steps C6 and C7.

C6 — entrypoint + graceful shutdown. Before writing it, I resolved a real gap that had been sitting unaddressed in the shared library: nothing gave a real adapter a stable signing identity — every existing connect() (test-only) generates a fresh random keypair per call, exactly the problem B7 diagnosed for concurrent connections, just recurring across restarts instead. crypto.py's own docstring already said the intended fix ("real adapters use the nkey nsc already generated... the same keypair serves both connection auth and event signing") — it just wasn't built. Added jetcore/creds.py (parses the nkey seed straight out of a real .creds file — caught a real quirk there too: nsc's template is asymmetric, 5 dashes on BEGIN, 6 on END) and BusClient.connect_as_adapter(), now the real entrypoint's connection path.

The encryption keypair is not solved the same way — still generated fresh per process start. I considered deriving it from the signing seed and rejected that (it would undermine §4.1's explicit "two independent keypairs" security rationale), and building real persistent storage for a second secret is its own design decision, not something to smuggle into an entrypoint step. Documented as a new, explicit §9 open item instead of quietly ignored.

Then actually ran python -m file_storage_adapter as a real process against the live stack — published a real command from a separate process, watched it write the file and publish the completion event; dropped a file directly into watch_dir and confirmed the external-creation path fired without a duplicate; sent a real SIGTERM and confirmed clean exit in under a second. Hit the encryption-keypair limitation firsthand along the way (a stale message from an old key failed to decrypt) — good confirmation the documented tradeoff is real, not hypothetical.

C7 — tests + verification. Wrote test_entrypoint.py, driving __main__.run() directly rather than re-exercising C4/C5's classes in isolation. Running the suite repeatedly turned up a second, genuinely intermittent bug in C4's own BusClient.fetch() fix: nats-py's JetStream fetch() has a second internal timeout path that raises the bare asyncio.TimeoutError rather than nats.errors.TimeoutError — so the earlier fix only caught the timeout it happened to hit first (~1-in-5 failure rate). Fixed by catching the builtin TimeoutError instead of the narrower subclass. Confirmed with 10 consecutive clean runs, not one.

101 tests total (up from 94), full suite run 4× for stability, clean ruff/mypy/bandit/pip-audit. Design.md, Dependencies.md updated; bus state cleaned; jetcore-nats-1 still healthy.

Track C (C1–C7) is done — the File Storage Adapter works standing alone, proven both by tests and by a real running process. Ready for Track D — the Webhook Listener whenever you'd like to continue.

