Track D (D1–D5) complete. Here's the summary:

D1 — scaffold. adapters/webhook_listener, applying Track C's lessons from the start: fastapi/uvicorn deferred until actually used, test files pre-emptively uniquely named.

D2 — settings. WebhookListenerSettings adds webhook_secret: SecretStr (masks the value from repr()/logs) and http_port. Hit the exact test_settings.py naming collision C1 predicted would recur — renamed before it could reach the full suite.

D3 — the HTTP endpoint. POST /webhooks/{path:path}: constant-time secret check via secrets.compare_digest, empty-path rejection, raw body → base64 content, fire-and-forget publish, 202. GET /healthz. Two real findings: TestClient only runs FastAPI's lifespan inside a with block — a bare instantiation silently never connects the bus client; and starlette 1.6.0 now prefers a genuinely separate package, httpx2, over httpx for its test client (confirmed by reading its import fallback, not assumed).

D4 — entrypoint. uvicorn.run() needs no hand-rolled signal handling, unlike C6's raw asyncio loop — it drains and shuts down gracefully on its own. Verified with a real running process again: real curl POSTs, a real signed+encrypted FileWriteRequested confirmed on the stream via raw nats stream get, and a real SIGTERM producing uvicorn's own clean shutdown sequence (confirming the lifespan actually closed the bus connection, not just that the process exited).

D5 — the live-bus proof. This one had a genuine bug worth flagging clearly: my first attempt reached into app.state.bus_client from the test's own event loop to pre-warm its recipient cache — TestClient runs the app's lifespan (and everything it creates) on a separate thread with its own event loop, so that hung silently for the full timeout. Fixed by not doing that at all: since RecipientCache.start() blocks until its catch-up snapshot loads, subscribing the observer before the app is even created is sufficient — no cross-loop access needed. Also hit a dumb but costly one on the way: forgot nats_url in the test's settings, silently falling back to the Docker-internal nats://nats:4222 hostname, which doesn't resolve from the host — several minutes of DNS-retry timeout before I caught it.

115 tests total, full suite run 3× clean, the new entrypoint test run 5× standalone given how it failed. Clean ruff/mypy/bandit/pip-audit throughout. Design.md and Dependencies.md updated with every real finding; bus state cleaned; jetcore-nats-1 still healthy.

