Track E complete — all four steps (E1–E4), and with it, Phase 2.

E1/E2 — Dockerfiles and Compose wiring. Both images built clean on the first real attempt (build context = repo root, since a uv workspace path-dependency needs the real libs/jetcore/ tree, confirmed by testing). Found a real issue running the built image: containers ran as root by default, so file-storage-adapter's writes to the host bind mount landed root-owned — couldn't even be deleted without sudo. Fixed with a non-root appuser (UID 1000) in both Dockerfiles, re-verified. Full docker compose up -d --build brought all three services up together; confirmed JetStream data survives a nats container recreation via the named volume.

E3 — manual smoke test, through real HTTP against the real stack: valid POST → file on host, correct content; wrong/missing secret → 401, nothing written; a ..-traversal in the URL was blocked by HTTP normalization before reaching the app, so I retried URL-encoded to actually exercise the code — the Webhook Listener accepted it (by design, it doesn't validate paths) but the File Storage Adapter's resolve_within rejected it, confirmed in its own logs, nothing written anywhere; confirmed FileCreateCompleted-then-FileWriteCompleted on a real repeat write to the same path.

E4 — the automated end-to-end test surfaced the most significant finding since Phase 1's ack-permission gap: an intermittent (~1-in-5-to-8) decrypt/signature failure in unrelated tests, root-caused to RegistryClient.heartbeat() — its first recipient-registration write fired asynchronously, so a quick subscribe()-then-close() could have close()'s cancellation land while that write was still in flight. Cancellation can't retract bytes already on the wire; the write would land later, sometimes after a different test had already purged and re-registered under the same identity, corrupting it. Fixed by making heartbeat() async and awaiting the first registration before the background loop starts. Alongside it: the shared NATS instance had accumulated 1223 leftover consumers across this whole session, and one failure showed a genuine server-side ServiceUnavailableError. Did a full infra rebuild too — 17 consecutive clean full-suite runs followed, up from a baseline that failed roughly 1 in 5–8.

116 tests total, clean ruff/mypy/bandit/pip-audit. Also caught and fixed a real doc-drift bug along the way — README's bandit command had never been updated to include webhook_listener since Track D. Compose stack (nats, webhook-listener, file-storage-adapter) confirmed healthy on a fresh, real end-to-end curl check after everything.

Phase 2 is done. The Webhook Listener → File Storage Adapter vertical slice works standing alone in tests, wired into docker-compose, and proven by hand through real HTTP — the pattern Phase 1 set out to prove now has two real adapters actually running it.

