Reads cleanly. Phase 3 is fully scoped and documented.

Phase 3 scoped — Design.md §13, five new Decisions (#23–27).

Scoping this surfaced real gaps that needed resolving before a coherent breakdown was possible, not just adapter-by-adapter bullet points:

REST API Service's "sync reply" was described in §8 but never actually mechanized. Decided against core NATS request-reply (the more obvious fit) because it would mean an unencrypted side-channel outside the uniform envelope/signing model every other event goes through — instead it reuses BusClient's existing pub/sub via an in-memory pending-request map, which requires a small, real jetcore addition: publish() needs to start returning the eventId it generates (currently discarded).
File Storage's list/read/delete needed a real answer for "file not found," and I drew a deliberate line: an expected business failure (not found) gets a real FileOperationFailed event back to the caller, while a security-relevant one (path traversal) stays a silent ack+log — the caller doesn't get confirmation or denial either way.
The Database Adapter needed an actual schema (one orders table, caller-supplied key so the write path can upsert without a round trip) and a way to close the loop back to a waiting REST API Service caller — a new OrderPersisted write-ack, deliberately separate from the CDC path's RowChanged output so "this request finished" and "the table changed" don't get conflated.
Webhook Sender / HTTP Adapter both needed a decision on where their outbound target comes from — settled as one fixed target per instance, reusing Decision #18's existing per-instance-identity pattern rather than inventing dynamic routing.
Six tracks (F–K): F extends the existing File Storage Adapter (no new infra), G/H are the two simple one-way adapters, I is the REST API Service (built against a fake replier so it doesn't need J to exist yet), J is the Database Adapter (the only track needing new infra — a real mysql service, binlog config, two least-privilege DB users), and K wires everything into docker-compose with a manual smoke test and one genuine cross-adapter automated test (REST API Service ↔ Database Adapter). Sequencing: F/G/H and J's infra can start in parallel; I's real end-to-end proof waits on J.

Also went back and fixed §9: item #1 (File Storage's command schema) is now fully resolved rather than partially; item #3 (external update-detection) is explicitly reconfirmed as staying deferred past Phase 3, not silently expanded into it.

