Track J — Database Adapter (MySQL): complete
All 7 steps built and empirically verified against real infra (no mocking of NATS or MySQL):

J1: mysql:8.4.11 wired into docker-compose.yml, binlog enabled, orders table + two least-privilege MySQL users via init.sql. Real finding: MySQL's ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE requires SELECT privilege on referenced columns even with the modern row-alias syntax — grant set corrected and documented.
J2–J3: package scaffold (jetcore-db-adapter-mysql, Decision #28 from the start) + DbAdapterSettings with two distinct MySQL identities (write vs. CDC).
J4: write-path upsert (OrderCreated → OrderPersisted), new schemas for both. Found asyncmy silently needs cryptography for MySQL 8.4's default auth plugin, and a real Step-B7-class test bug (two connections under one serviceId clobbering each other's identity).
J5: CDC read path via python-mysql-replication. Two real, non-obvious library findings shaped the design: blocking=True can't be interrupted from another thread (hangs forever — confirmed empirically), and a fresh reader starts from the earliest retained binlog, not "now" (new open item, §9 #6).
J6–J7: entrypoint gathering write path + CDC watcher concurrently; entrypoint test proves one OrderCreated produces both OrderPersisted and RowChanged from the same running process. Manual verification included a real direct-SQL write bypassing the bus and a clean SIGTERM.
229 tests total (25 new), clean ruff/mypy --all-packages/bandit/pip-audit.

The significant finding this track: the "known flaky pattern" tracked since Step E4 finally got a real root cause, not just another data point. It isn't primarily NATS consumer-count strain — it's that docker compose's always-running file-storage-adapter/webhook-listener containers use a stable signing identity while test suites connect under the same serviceId with a fresh key every call; the no-TTL service-identity directory means whichever registers last wins. Isolated conclusively: stopping those containers took the previously-flaky test from 2-of-3 failures to 5-for-5 clean, and the full suite from 1-in-3 failures to 2-for-2 clean. Documented in full in Design.md §9 item #5, deliberately not fixed at the root here — recorded precisely for whichever track addresses it next rather than re-diagnosed again.

Design.md §13/§9 and Dependencies.md are all updated (including testcontainers-python reconsidered-but-not-needed, and tenacity deliberately not adopted for the write path). Bus and DB state purged, stack left running. Ready for Track K (Integration) whenever you want to proceed.

will the recurring issue impact a production service when new adapters are introduced?
Good question — and the honest answer is: partially yes, and Track K is about to make it worse before anyone fixes it, but the mechanism is narrower than "any two adapters can collide." Let me separate what's actually going on.

What the observed failures actually require
Both conditions have to hold at once:

A stable-identity process already registered as a given serviceId (a real adapter running via connect_as_adapter(), whose signing key is derived from its .creds file).
Something else connecting under the same serviceId with a different signing key, which overwrites the first in the no-TTL service-identity KV (Decision #20: "whichever registers last wins, no revocation beyond overwrite").
Right now, condition 2 is caused entirely by this project's own test suites reusing real adapter identities (file-storage-01, webhook-listener-01) for testing convenience via ad-hoc connect() calls with fresh random keys — not by two independent production adapters. Decision #18's per-instance identity convention means two different production adapter instances never share a serviceId in the first place, so today, in the actual running stack (no tests involved), this doesn't fire.

Where it gets worse as adapters are introduced
Track K is exactly the track that changes this. Right now only file-storage-adapter and webhook-listener are long-running Compose containers — webhook-sender, http-adapter, rest-api-service, and db-adapter-mysql aren't deployed yet, so their own test suites (which use the identical connect()-with-a-fresh-key pattern under webhook-sender-01, http-adapter-01, etc.) have never collided with anything, because nothing with a stable identity has been running under those serviceIds to collide with.

Once K1/K2 give all four of those adapters their own always-running containers, every one of their own test suites becomes exposed to the same class of failure that file_storage_adapter/webhook_listener have been hitting — not a new bug, but the existing gap surfacing in four more places simultaneously. That's a direct, concrete "yes" to your question for this specific manifestation.

The deeper production risk, independent of testing
Separately, Decision #20's "no revocation beyond overwrite" is a real gap on its own merits, not just a testing artifact. In an actual production deployment (no tests anywhere), the same overwrite-wins model would still be exposed to:

A rolling deploy where an old instance lingers briefly alongside a new one under a rotated credential (same serviceId, deliberately different key) — the loser's in-flight messages become unverifiable.
Worse: nothing currently stops any process that can get a valid NATS credential issued for an existing serviceId from registering its own signing key and having the system start trusting it as that adapter — a real impersonation path, not just a flakiness one.
More adapters and more deployments mean more credential-rotation and restart events overall, so the aggregate exposure to this class of problem grows with adapter count even though no two adapters' identities ever legitimately overlap by design.

What would actually need to change
Two separate fixes, not one:

For the test-flakiness manifestation: stop letting test suites impersonate real adapter identities — give tests their own distinct serviceIds (or route through connect_as_adapter()'s stable identity instead of a fresh one) so they never contend with a real container for the same registration.
For the underlying production gap: service-identity needs an actual revocation/expiry story (TTL + heartbeat like service-directory already has, or an explicit admin-driven revoke) before this project is safe to run as anything beyond a demo — this is exactly what Decision #20 already flagged as unresolved, not a new finding.
Neither is fixed today; both are recorded (§9 items, Decision #20's own caveat) rather than addressed. Want me to add a Track K-scoped note flagging that its container rollout will surface the test-identity collision in the other four adapters' suites, so it doesn't get "rediscovered" as a fresh mystery there?

yes
