Track K — Integration: complete. Phase 3 is complete.
All 4 steps built and verified against the real, fully-wired stack:

K1: four more Dockerfiles (webhook_sender, http_adapter, rest_api_service, db_adapter_mysql). Real correction to Step E2's own documented reasoning: with the workspace now at 8 members, uv sync --frozen --package X only needs X's own dependency subgraph present, not every sibling's pyproject.toml — confirmed by testing, so the four new Dockerfiles use a simpler, minimal copy set than the two originals.
K2: all 6 adapters + mysql wired into docker-compose.yml, real .creds mounted, deliberately in-network (never unreachable, never a no-op) demo targets — Webhook Sender relays into the Webhook Listener for real, HTTP Adapter POSTs to /healthz for a real 405. All 8 services built and started healthy together.
K3: one manual smoke-test script against the live stack — File Storage list/read/delete, one real POST fanning out to three independent consumers at once (Webhook Sender, HTTP Adapter, Database Adapter's sync-reply), and a direct SQL write proving CDC is real. All four parts passed on the first run.
K4: tests/test_rest_api_service_to_db_adapter_end_to_end.py — the REST API Service ↔ Database Adapter sync-reply pair, mirroring Step E4's in-process-real-entrypoints pattern. Passes standing alone.
Exit criteria met — K3 and K4 each demonstrated a real, passing run against the complete stack.

The one significant thing to flag: K2 triggered exactly the consequence I flagged in advance last session. With all 6 adapters now long-running containers, the §9 item #5 identity-collision issue (documented from Track J) went from affecting 1-2 adapters' test suites to 5 simultaneous failures across 4 adapters in a single full-suite run — confirmed via the same stop/rerun isolation (230/230 clean with containers stopped). Two refinements this track added to that finding: it now also reaches Step E4's long-standing cross-adapter test and the new K4 test, and it manifests through both identity directories (not just the no-TTL signing-key one — the TTL'd encryption-key directory races the same way via normal heartbeat timing). Design.md §9 item #5 is updated with the full mechanism and scale; not fixed at the root, same as before, but the case for prioritizing a real fix is now considerably stronger given how much more of the suite it touches.

Design.md's Track K checklist, §9, Dependencies.md are all current. This closes out the whole Phase 3 breakdown — every adapter type in §8 is now built, tested, containerized, and proven working together. Let me know what's next.

