before proceeding, lets review the Open Questions in and see if is anything we need to address.
Here's a review of §9's six items, with my assessment of what's actually actionable now versus fine to keep deferring:

No action needed
#1 (File Storage schema) — already resolved, just a stale numbering anchor.
#2 (subject naming placeholders) — deliberate, ongoing design choice (Decision #14). Nothing in Phase 3/4/5's plan depends on resolving this; it's meant to stay a placeholder for this project's scope.
#3 (externally-triggered file updates) — narrow, low-priority, genuinely out of scope for what's been built.
Real gaps, correctly deferred, no reason to touch now
#4 (encryption keypair not persisted across restarts) — a genuine production-readiness gap ("worth resolving before any real production deployment"), but it doesn't block anything in this project's own remaining plan (§10 Phase 4 = CI/schemas/testcontainers, Phase 5 = finalize docker-compose). Only matters if you're heading toward real deployment, not toward finishing this project's stated scope.
#6 (CDC read position not persisted) — same category, same reasoning, lower stakes (a demo-scope restart just replays history rather than losing anything).
The one I'd actually recommend addressing now
#5 (test-identity collision). Here's why this one is different from #4/#6: it's not a "would matter in real production" gap — it's actively costing test-suite reliability right now, and it's about to matter more, not less:

§10 Phase 4 explicitly plans GitHub Actions CI next. A ~2%-of-tests intermittent-failure rate, concentrated exactly in entrypoint tests, is a bad foundation to build CI on — every red run will need manual triage to distinguish "real regression" from "known collision," which erodes trust in CI fast.
The mechanism is well-understood now (not a mystery to chase), which means a fix is actually scoped, not open-ended.
There are two different levels of fix, worth separating:

Cheap, immediate, zero-risk: CI shouldn't bring up the six adapter application containers before running pytest in the first place — only nats+mysql are actually needed for the test suite itself (the containers exist to prove deployment works, which is K3's manual-smoke-test job, not pytest's). If CI is scoped that way from the start, this collision likely never fires in CI at all. This is really a Phase 4 CI-design decision, not a code change.
Real fix, bigger: give service-identity an actual revocation/TTL story (Decision #20's already-flagged future work), so a stale registration can't silently and permanently shadow a live one. This is the "worth doing eventually" fix, not urgent today.
My recommendation: don't chase #4/#6 now (correctly deferred, not on the critical path), but do keep #5 in mind explicitly when we scope Phase 4's CI workflow — specifically, design the CI job to start only nats+mysql, not the full adapter fleet, before running pytest. That's a decision worth making deliberately rather than defaulting into "bring the whole stack up" and rediscovering this the hard way in CI logs.

Want me to fold that CI-scoping decision into Design.md now (as a forward note on Phase 4, mirroring how I flagged K2's consequence in advance), or hold off until we're actually scoping Phase 4?

