------------------ Defect 1 Resolved ------------------

Since we've identified Defect 2, let's see if we can reproduce and determine a root cause.
Let me look at registry.py's actual cache-fill guarantee first — it turns out RecipientCache.start() blocks until a genuinely complete initial snapshot, which argues against my original "stale cache at connect time" theory. A more likely mechanism, given this session's well-documented server-strain history: the real containers' 20s heartbeat occasionally missing its 60s TTL deadline under heavy test load, causing their recipient entry to briefly expire — a real, temporary absence, not a caching bug. Let me test that directly by polling a real container's KV entry while generating realistic load.

Now let's generate real load — a full pytest run — while the poller runs concurrently.

Confirmed, decisively. The pattern is unambiguous: http-adapter-01's entry vanished for exactly ~8 seconds (01:57:24–31) then recovered; db-adapter-mysql-01's vanished for 59 seconds straight (01:57:32–58:31). This isn't heartbeat jitter under load — it's active deletion. Let me confirm the exact mechanism in the code.

Let's directly correlate this with the real containers' own error logs from that same window, to close the loop completely.

Confirmed — errors on both containers during exactly the windows their KV registration was deleted. Root cause is fully closed. Let's update Defects.md with the confirmed mechanism.

Now let's rewrite this section with the confirmed root cause.

Now let's update the summary table's Severity/Status wording and Design.md's cross-reference for accuracy.

Now let's update Design.md's item #7 to match the confirmed root cause instead of the earlier "working theory."

All 8 services healthy, no crashes from the investigation, bus state clean. Here's the finding:

Defect 2 — root cause confirmed
Not the cache-propagation theory I originally guessed — I checked registry.py's own implementation first and it argues against that (RecipientCache.start() genuinely blocks until a complete initial snapshot; a fresh cache is accurate the moment it's usable).

The real mechanism, reproduced deterministically: five different conftest.py files (db_adapter_mysql, rest_api_service, webhook_sender, http_adapter, and the root tests/) each have an autouse _clean_state fixture that blanket-deletes every service-directory KV entry under a shared subject prefix (events.orders.OrderCreated.*) before every single test — with no filter distinguishing test identities from real ones. That was always safe when no real adapter was registered under that subject; it stopped being safe the moment Step K2 made three real adapters permanently, legitimately registered there.

I confirmed this directly by polling a real container's KV entry once a second (bypassing any cache) during a normal pytest run:

http-adapter-01: gone for 8 straight seconds, then recovered on its next heartbeat.
db-adapter-mysql-01: gone for 59 straight seconds — its own 26-test suite alone re-triggers the delete faster than any heartbeat can catch up.
Cross-checked against docker compose logs for that exact window: 20 and 13 decrypt/verify errors respectively, concentrated precisely there — not spread evenly through the run. A message published during one of these gaps is permanently unencryptable-for-that-recipient (a later heartbeat can't retroactively fix an already-published ciphertext), and with no max_deliver cap configured anywhere, it just redelivers forever.

I've written the full mechanism into Defects.md (including the ruled-out theory, so it isn't reconsidered from scratch later) and cross-linked Design.md §9 item #7. I didn't design or apply a fix — that wasn't this turn's ask — but the shape is obvious given the confirmed cause: scope those deletes to test-only serviceIds instead of deleting indiscriminately by subject, and a max_deliver cap would independently bound the blast radius regardless. Want me to proceed with that fix the same way we did Defect 1?

