Summary
Defect 2 — Fixed and Verified. Two changes:

All 8 conftest.py files' _clean_state fixtures now scope their service-directory KV cleanup to test-only identities (-test suffix / test- prefix) instead of blanket-deleting every registered recipient for a shared subject — never touching a real adapter's registration.
Added a max_deliver=5 cap to bus_client.py's _consumer_config(), completing what the module's own docstring already described as intended but was never actually configured — bounds any remaining undecryptable-message case to a few attempts instead of forever.
Verified directly: polled a real container's KV entry once a second through an entire 26-test suite — its registeredAt timestamp never changed, confirming it was never deleted.

Along the way, I also completed Defect 1's rollout properly — found that libs/jetcore/tests/test_bus_client.py (Track B's own foundational tests) had been missed by the original file search entirely, still connecting as bare webhook-listener-01/file-storage-01. Fixed the same way as the other 15 files.

Fixing Defect 2 surfaced a third, real problem — logged as Defect 3, not fixed. Making the real containers reliably present exposed that -test identities share identical permissions with their real counterparts (Defect 1's own design choice), so a real adapter and its test twin are both legitimate subscribers to the same placeholder subject. A test's own trigger now reliably also triggers the real container's real behavior, racing test assertions or invalidating stated preconditions. This caused 8 real pytest failures in the very next run — a genuine regression in observed reliability, even though neither Defect 1 nor Defect 2's fix is wrong on its own terms.

I stopped there rather than picking a fix unilaterally, since Defect 3 needs a real design decision — I've written up four candidate directions in Defects.md (permission asymmetry, separate test-only subjects, marker-based assertions, or not running the fleet during local test runs), each with real tradeoffs. ruff/mypy/bandit are clean, all 8 containers are healthy, and Design.md §9 and Defects.md are fully updated and cross-linked.

The suite currently sits at 222/230 passing — the 8 failures are understood, documented, and all attributable to Defect 3. Want to go through those options and decide how to proceed?

------------------ Defect 2 Resolved ------------------

Let's go through the options for resolving Defect 3
Let me walk through each option with the real tradeoffs, then give a recommendation.

Option A: Reduce -test identities' subscribe permissions
Doesn't actually work. Most of the affected tests use the -test identity as the adapter under test — it fundamentally needs subscribe permission on the subject it's proving. Removing that permission breaks the test outright rather than fixing the race. Not viable.

Option B: Separate test-only subjects
Real adapters and -test identities would publish/subscribe on disjoint subjects (e.g., events.orders.OrderCreated for real traffic vs. a test-scoped variant), so a real container literally never sees a test message. Architecturally the cleanest fix — it removes the race at the root, and since every handler class takes subject as a plain constructor argument (nothing is hardcoded to a specific subject string), it still exercises the exact same code path.

Cost: touches every adapter's _xyz_helpers.py subject constants, plus new manifest permission entries — a moderate, mechanical refactor on the same scale as Defect 1's rollout. It also raises a real question for K3/K4, which deliberately want to prove the real subject against the real deployed system — those would need to keep using the real subject, so this fix would apply selectively (handler/entrypoint tests only), not universally.

Option C: Marker-based, filter-tolerant assertions
Stop assuming [result] = await observer.fetch(...) sees exactly one message; poll and filter for a message matching a unique test-generated marker instead (the pattern this project already uses in test_cdc_watcher.py and K3's manual script). Zero infrastructure changes.

Cost: touches assertion logic in every affected test file — broader than Option B in some ways, since it's a per-test-file code change, not a config change. And it has a real gap: test_publish_with_no_recipients_does_not_crash asserts a precondition ("nobody is registered yet"), not a result — no marker can fix an assertion whose premise is now structurally false. That test would need Option B or D regardless.

Option D: Don't run the real fleet during local test runs
Stop the six adapter containers before running the full pytest suite (matching the CI practice already recommended under Defect 1); leave nats+mysql up. Zero code changes — pure workflow.

Cost: loses the "stack always running" convenience for casual manual poking during a pytest run. Solvable with a thin wrapper script (stop adapters → pytest → restart adapters) so the friction is automated away, not manual discipline.

Recommendation: D as the primary fix, informed by B
Here's the reasoning: the 8 currently-failing tests (and every other handler/entrypoint test like them) were never designed to depend on a co-running production fleet — they're proving one adapter's own code against real NATS/MySQL infrastructure, in isolation. The race is an accident of this dev session's habit of leaving the fleet running throughout, not a deliberate design goal anywhere in Design.md. Option D removes the entire class of problem (not just these 8 tests, but every future test that happens to touch a shared subject) with zero code risk, and makes local dev consistent with the CI practice already recommended.

It doesn't cost K3/K4 anything — I checked: K4's sync-reply test is inherently immune already (REST API Service's own PendingReplies keys strictly by correlationId, so it's marker-filtered by construction, not by luck), and K3 is a deliberate, manual "prove it against the real deployed thing" step that's supposed to run with the fleet up.

I'd skip B (real but the highest-cost option here, and D removes the need for it) and skip C as the primary fix (touches more files than D for a narrower fix, and can't resolve the one precondition-based test regardless) — but C's filtering pattern is worth keeping in mind for K3/K4-style tests specifically, if this project ever adds one that does need the fleet running and isn't already correlation-filtered by design.

Want me to proceed with D — a wrapper script that stops the six adapter containers, runs pytest, and restarts them — as the actual fix?

Proceed with recommended fix "D"
