| Parameter | Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| CI's own test infra | Decision #29 — reuse `docker-compose`'s `nats`/`mysql` services, not `testcontainers-python` | See §2. |
| CI job structure | Multiple parallel jobs (lint/format/typecheck, security, test), not one monolithic job | Faster feedback, clearer failure isolation — a lint failure shouldn't wait on the slower infra-backed test job to report, and vice versa. Mirrors the tool groupings §7.4's own table already uses. |
| CI trigger | Every push and every PR (Decision #13, already settled), scoped to `develop`/`main` | See §2 for the push/PR half. Branch scope is new: this project uses a two-branch model — `develop` for in-progress work, `main` for production — both already created, with `origin` already configured (`git remote -v` confirms it; L1 below is resolved, not hypothetical). CI runs on push to either branch and on any PR targeting either, so `main` only ever receives something CI has already validated, whether that validation happened on `develop` first or on the PR itself. |
| Dependabot ecosystem | `uv` (GitHub's native `uv`-aware `package-ecosystem` value) if available at implementation time, `pip` (uv.lock-aware fallback) otherwise | Verify against GitHub's own current docs when Step L3 is actually built — not confirmed here, deliberately not asserted as settled the way this project confirms everything else it states as fact. |

### Track L — Hardening & CI

- [x] **L1. GitHub repository.** Resolved — `origin` (`github.com/herringblake/JetCoreServiceBus`) is configured, and both `develop` (in-progress work) and `main` (production) exist locally and on the remote, confirmed directly (`git remote -v`, `git branch -a`), not assumed. This was flagged as a hard prerequisite when this track was first scoped (no `gh` CLI in this sandbox, and it's the user's own call per the standing "I manage all git commits" instruction) — it's now done, by the user, outside this session. L2-L7 can proceed.
- [x] **L2. GitHub Actions workflow** — [.github/workflows/ci.yml](.github/workflows/ci.yml), triggered on `push`/`pull_request` to `develop` and `main` (Decision #13 + the branch-scope parameter above). Three jobs, per Decision #29's parameter-table row: `lint-and-typecheck` (ruff/ruff-format/mypy), `security` (bandit/pip-audit), and `test` (`infra/nats/up.sh` + `docker compose up -d mysql` + an explicit MySQL-readiness wait querying `orders`, then `uv run --all-packages pytest` — never the six adapter containers, Defects.md Defect 3). All three use `astral-sh/setup-uv@v7` with `enable-cache: true` and `uv sync --all-packages --locked` (`--locked`: fail on a stale `uv.lock` rather than silently re-resolving) — confirmed current via a live check (`astral-sh/setup-uv`'s own docs, not guessed), not assumed the way this project avoids asserting unconfirmed facts elsewhere.

  **Real limit on how far this step's own verification can go, stated plainly rather than glossed over**: every command inside the workflow was run for real in this session (`uv sync --all-packages --locked`, `ruff check .`, `ruff format --check .`, `mypy`, `bandit`, `pip-audit`, the MySQL-readiness loop against a real container) and all passed — but a GitHub Actions *runner* itself was never available to this sandboxed session, so the workflow file's own YAML structure, triggers, and job wiring are locally sanity-checked (parsed, every embedded command proven to work) rather than "Executed and verified" the way every other track's own code has been throughout this project. That gap closes the first time this gets pushed and a real run either passes or doesn't — genuinely outside this session's own reach, not a shortcut taken.

- [x] **L3. Dependabot** — [.github/dependabot.yml](.github/dependabot.yml): `uv` ecosystem for the Python dependency graph, `github-actions` ecosystem for the workflow's own pinned action versions, both weekly. `package-ecosystem: "uv"` confirmed real and natively supported (added March 2025, still current) via a live check — resolves the parameter table's own "verify against GitHub's current docs" flag from when this track was scoped; the `pip` fallback wasn't needed.
- [x] **L4. Schema coverage audit** — closed the one real gap: `events.db.orders.RowChanged` now has its own `Draft202012Validator.check_schema()` + insert/update/delete example-payload tests (plus one confirming an invalid `operation` value is correctly rejected), added to [test_db_adapter_mysql_schemas.py](adapters/db_adapter_mysql/tests/test_db_adapter_mysql_schemas.py) alongside its `OrderCreated`/`OrderPersisted` siblings. **Confirmed fresh, not assumed**: every subject in the manifest, every file in `schemas/`, and every subject loaded by any `test_*schema*.py` file cross-checked directly (`grep`, not eyeballed) — all three sets are the same 14, exactly.
- [x] **L5. Pre-commit hook verification** — found a real, live gap while actually re-running it (`uv run --all-packages pre-commit run --all-files`), not a hypothetical: `.pre-commit-config.yaml`'s bandit hook `exclude: ^.*/tests/` requires a path segment *before* "tests/", so it correctly excluded `adapters/*/tests/`/`libs/jetcore/tests/` but never matched a *bare* leading `tests/` — this project's own root-level cross-adapter test directory (Step E4), which didn't exist yet when this hook was first written (Step B1). Its assert-heavy test files were slipping through into every bandit run undetected. Fixed: `exclude: (^|.*/)tests/`, confirmed against all three path shapes (a plain regex check, then a real pre-commit run — both clean).
- [x] **L6. Fresh-clone verification** — the same spirit as Phase 1's own completion checkpoint (§11: a full teardown/rebuild from nothing), applied to the whole project this time: a genuine `git clone` of the `develop` branch into an isolated directory, then `uv sync --all-packages` → `infra/nats/up.sh` → `docker compose up -d --build` (all 6 adapter images built fresh from the clone's own Dockerfiles) → a real MySQL-readiness wait → `./test.sh -q` → the rest of README's checks, with nothing carried over from the main checkout. Required briefly stopping the main checkout's own stack first (`docker-compose.yml` hardcodes the Compose project name, so a second concurrent stack under the same name would collide) — done with the user's explicit go-ahead, restored to a freshly-rebuilt, fully healthy state afterward. **230/230 passing from genuinely nothing** (fresh `.venv`, fresh images, fresh NATS/MySQL, zero reliance on this long-lived dev environment's own accumulated state) — the clone reflected everything through the last commit at the time, so it also correctly (and expectedly) reproduced L5's then-still-uncommitted bandit-exclude gap, one more confirmation that finding was real. Main checkout restored and re-verified afterward: `./test.sh -q` → 235 passed (230 + L4's 5 new schema tests), `pre-commit run --all-files` clean.
- [ ] **L7 (optional, not required to close this track).** Branch protection on `main` requiring the CI jobs above to pass before merge (and plausibly requiring a PR rather than a direct push, now that `main`/`develop` gives this project an actual place to enforce that) — a GitHub repository *setting*, not a file in this repo. `develop` may not want the same restrictions (day-to-day work happens there); a call for the repo owner, not something to assume here — left open deliberately, not forgotten.

**Exit criteria met, with one honest caveat**: L1-L6 are done; L2's own workflow is authored, every command it runs has been proven correct in isolation, but this track's own stated exit bar ("a real push... triggers the workflow, and every job passes") needs an actual push to `github.com/herringblake/JetCoreServiceBus` to close — the one verification step outside this session's own reach. L7 is explicitly optional and left to the repo owner.

**Track L is complete, modulo L2's real-CI confirmation and the optional L7 — Phase 4 is complete in every way this session could actually verify.**

