Here are the search results for your NATS JetStream query:

## Key Findings

**Message Deduplication:**
Streams support deduplication using a Nats-Msg-Id header and a sliding window within which to track duplicate messages. The default window to track duplicates in is 2 minutes, this can be set on the command line using --dupe-window when creating streams.

**Multiple Filter Subjects in Consumers:**
A set of subjects that overlap with the subjects bound to the stream to filter delivery to subscribers. This cannot be used with the FilterSubject field. Additionally, support for multiple filter consumers is available when using nats-server +v2.10, which is only supported when using the pull_subscribe_bind API.

**Consumer Filter Configuration:**
The –filter value is the original subject the eligibility result is published to, while the –target value is what the subscriber will use to consume messages from the consumer.

**How Deduplication Works:**
Publishers can set a unique message ID, and JetStream will reject duplicates within a configurable window.

These results show that NATS JetStream provides robust deduplication capabilities through the Nats-Msg-Id header combined with a configurable time window, and modern versions support multiple filter subjects for consumers.


