Good question — I went and checked the live server config rather than going from memory, since this project always verifies rather than assumes. A few real, concrete things stand out.

Directly relevant to gaps already on record
1. Redelivery backoff — not set, and it bears directly on Defect 2. bus_client.py's _consumer_config() sets max_deliver=5 but no ack_wait/backoff, so every redelivery attempt waits the flat 30s server default. ConsumerConfig.backoff is a real field in the pinned client (nats-py>=2.15.0 — confirmed by introspecting it directly). Defect 2's own evidence was 8-59 second registration gaps; a backoff like [2s, 5s, 15s, 30s, 60s] would let the first retry land almost immediately — much likelier to catch a transient gap closing — while still backing off if the problem persists. Small, additive, no schema/behavior change elsewhere.

2. Message dedup — already provisioned on every stream, never actually used. I checked live: EVENTS, service-directory, and service-identity all already have a duplicate_window set (120s / 60s / 120s — NATS's own defaults from nats stream add/kv add). But nothing in bus_client.py's publish() ever sets a Nats-Msg-Id header — so the capability is paid for and sitting idle. It would matter the moment a client retries an HTTP call to REST API Service or Webhook Listener after a network blip that actually succeeded server-side (a duplicate OrderCreated/FileWriteRequested publish, no way for downstream to know it's a dupe). Nothing currently supplies an idempotency key to plumb through, so this is latent rather than an active bug — but it's a real gap in the two HTTP front doors, and the infra is already there waiting.

3. Per-key TTL (allow_msg_ttl) on service-identity — a narrower angle on an already-flagged limitation. Confirmed live: not enabled on any bucket. Decision #20 and Defect 1 both flag service-identity's "no revocation beyond overwrite" as future hardening, but the option actually evaluated and rejected there was a heartbeat-refreshed TTL (correctly rejected — it doesn't fix the dual-live-instance race, just changes the flip-flop cadence). Per-key TTL is a different shape: a set-once, long expiry (say 90 days) on each identity registration would let a genuinely decommissioned identity eventually age out with zero ongoing cost — pure garbage collection, never actually evaluated on its own terms since it got bundled with the rejected idea.

4. KV --history 1 on both buckets — currently destroys its own forensic evidence. Decision #20's whole risk is "whichever registration lands last simply overwrites the previous one, permanently" — with history 1, there's nothing left to even investigate after the fact. Bumping to --history 5 or so is a one-line change to bootstrap_jetstream.sh, no code change (nats-py's KeyValue.history() already exists), small storage cost. Doesn't fix the race, but currently a collision is un-investigable after the fact, and that's fixable for free.

Available, but not clearly worth adopting here
filter_subjects (multi-subject consumers, NATS 2.10+/nats-py confirmed supported) — could collapse File Storage Adapter's 4 durable consumers into 1. But several handler docstrings explicitly call out "each subject gets its own durable consumer, per §6's design" as deliberate (independent concurrency/backpressure per subject) — consolidating trades that away. An option, not obviously a win.
max_ack_pending — unset (server default 1000). No evidence this project has ever gotten near that ceiling.
JetStream Object Store — could back File Storage Adapter instead of the host bind-mount. Interesting for a real distributed deployment, but that adapter's whole stated purpose is local file ops — this would be redesigning it, not extending it.
republish, mirrors/sources, num_replicas>1 — all real, all deliberately unused: this is explicitly a single-node dev/demo bus (--replicas 1 throughout), matching Phase 5's own scope. Not a gap.
Items 1 and 4 are cheap and low-risk enough to just do; 2 and 3 both imply a small design decision (where does an idempotency key come from; what's the right TTL value and who sets it) worth a short discussion first — want me to write these up as new Design.md Open Questions/Decisions, or pick one to scope out now?

Let's write them up as Open Questions
