7. **Ambient test traffic occasionally undecryptable by the real adapter containers — a distinct issue from item #5, now fixed.** With item #5 fixed (identity collision, no longer in play), the real containers' own logs still showed `pyrage.DecryptError`/`SignatureVerificationError` from ordinary test-generated traffic on subjects they legitimately subscribe to. Root cause, reproduced directly (polling a real container's `service-directory` KV entry once per second during a normal pytest run, bypassing any client-side cache): eight different `conftest.py` files' `_clean_state` fixtures blanket-deleted *every* KV entry under a shared subject prefix before every test — including the real containers' own registrations, not just test identities — and a deleted entry doesn't come back until that container's next 20s heartbeat. Confirmed absences of 8-59 consecutive seconds, correlated directly against real `DecryptError`/`SignatureVerificationError` log entries in that same window. **Fixed**: those fixtures now only delete keys belonging to test-only identities, plus a `max_deliver` cap added to `_consumer_config()` as a backstop. Verified: a real container's KV entry survived a full 26-test suite run with its `registeredAt` timestamp unchanged. **Full mechanism and applied fix: [Defects.md#defect-2-ambient-test-traffic-undecryptable-by-real-containers](Defects.md#defect-2-ambient-test-traffic-undecryptable-by-real-containers).**
8. **Real adapters react to shared-subject test traffic — surfaced by fixing item #7, now fixed.** Once items #5/#7's fixes made the real adapter fleet reliably live and registered (rather than intermittently disabled by either bug), a structural issue that both bugs had been partially masking became fully visible: `-test` identities were deliberately given identical permissions to their real counterparts (item #5's own design), so a real adapter and its `-test` twin are both legitimate, simultaneous subscribers to the same "placeholder" subject — meaning a test's own published trigger also reliably triggered the real container's real behavior, racing the test's own assertions or invalidating a test's stated precondition (e.g. "no recipients registered yet," no longer true once a real container is always registered). Caused 8 real pytest failures in the very next full-suite run after items #5/#7 were fixed. **Fixed**: [test.sh](test.sh) stops the six adapter containers (leaving `nats`/`mysql` up) before running pytest, restarting them afterward regardless of outcome — removes the whole class of problem rather than patching the 8 tests that happened to surface it first. Verified: 3 consecutive `./test.sh` runs, 230/230 passing each time. **Full mechanism and applied fix: [Defects.md#defect-3-real-adapters-react-to-shared-subject-test-traffic](Defects.md#defect-3-real-adapters-react-to-shared-subject-test-traffic).**
9. **No redelivery backoff configured — every retry waits the flat server-default AckWait (30s), including the first.** Found during a JetStream feature audit (post-Phase 5), not a build step: `bus_client.py`'s `_consumer_config()` sets `max_deliver=5` (Defect 2's own fix) but never sets `ack_wait`/`backoff`, so a genuinely transient failure (the exact shape Defect 2 diagnosed — 8-59 second registration gaps) waits a full 30s before its first retry even attempts to land inside the window where the problem may have already resolved. `ConsumerConfig.backoff: list[float]` is a real, present field in the pinned client (`nats-py>=2.15.0`, confirmed via direct introspection of the installed library, not docs) — an escalating schedule (e.g. `[2, 5, 15, 30, 60]` seconds) would let the first retry happen fast while still backing off for a persistent problem. Purely additive (one new field on an existing `ConsumerConfig` call); no schema or behavior change elsewhere. Deferred, not blocking — worth adopting, not yet decided on a concrete schedule.
10. **JetStream message deduplication is already paid for on every stream and completely unused.** Found the same audit: live `stream info` on `EVENTS`, `service-directory`, and `service-identity` all show a `duplicate_window` already set (120s/60s/120s — NATS's own `nats stream add`/`kv add` defaults), but nothing in `bus_client.py`'s `publish()` ever sets the `Nats-Msg-Id` header that capability keys off — confirmed by direct inspection of the one `publish()` call site. Currently latent rather than an active bug: nothing in this project produces a client-supplied idempotency key today. It would become a real gap the moment a caller retries a `POST /api/orders` (REST API Service) or `POST /webhooks/{path}` (Webhook Listener) after a network blip that actually succeeded server-side — a second, indistinguishable publish, with no way for any downstream consumer to know it's a duplicate. Needs a design decision before it's actionable: where the idempotency key comes from (a caller-supplied HTTP header? an adapter-derived hash of the body?) and which of the two HTTP front doors actually need it. Deferred, not blocking.
11. **Per-key TTL (`allow_msg_ttl`) on `service-identity` — a narrower angle on Decision #20's flagged limitation, not previously evaluated on its own terms.** Confirmed live (`stream info`): `allow_msg_ttl` is not enabled on any bucket today. Decision #20 and [Defects.md Defect 1](Defects.md#defect-1-testproduction-identity-collision) both flag `service-identity`'s "no revocation beyond overwrite" as future hardening, and Defect 1's own "Considered and not applied" section rejected *a heartbeat-refreshed TTL matching `service-directory`'s own model* — correctly, since re-heartbeating doesn't fix the dual-live-instance race, it just changes the flip-flop cadence. Per-key TTL is a different, cheaper mechanism that was never itself evaluated: a long, set-once expiry (e.g. 90 days) on each identity registration, refreshed only if the adapter ever reconnects, would let a genuinely decommissioned identity age out on its own with zero ongoing cost — pure garbage collection, not a fix for the concurrent-registration race (item stays open for that half). Deferred, not blocking — needs a concrete TTL value and confirmation this doesn't complicate a long-lived production adapter that legitimately never reconnects.
12. **KV `--history 1` on both buckets destroys the only evidence a collision under Decision #20 would leave behind.** Found the same audit. Decision #20's stated risk is "whichever registration lands last simply overwrites the previous one, permanently" — with `--history 1` (both buckets, [infra/nats/bootstrap_jetstream.sh](infra/nats/bootstrap_jetstream.sh)), the overwritten prior value isn't retrievable even moments later to confirm a suspected collision actually happened. Bumping to `--history 5` (or similar) is a one-line change to that script, no application code change (`nats-py`'s `KeyValue.history()` already exists), a small, bounded storage cost. Doesn't fix the underlying race — pure post-hoc observability. Deferred, not blocking; the only real question is what history depth is worth the storage cost, not whether to do it.

Item #4 in the original numbering here (the sender-authentication gap found in Step B6) is **resolved** — see Decision #20 and §4.6. It's noted there as a v1-scoped mechanism (trusts whoever registers first for a serviceId, no revocation beyond overwrite), not a fully solved problem for all time, but it's no longer an open design question — it's a documented, implemented, tested decision. (Renumbered above to avoid two different "item #4"s in this document — the encryption-keypair item is the new one.)

---
Added as items #9-12 in Design.md §9, each grounded in the live server config I checked (not restated from memory), and cross-linked to the existing decisions/defects they extend:

#9 — no redelivery backoff configured; every retry waits the flat 30s default, including the first — bears directly on Defect 2's own 8-59s gap evidence.
#10 — dedup (duplicate_window) is already live on all three streams; nothing ever sets Nats-Msg-Id, so it's unused capacity, latent until an HTTP front door needs idempotent retries.
#11 — per-key TTL on service-identity, framed explicitly as narrower than (and not the same as) the heartbeat-TTL idea Defect 1 already correctly rejected — a fresh angle on Decision #20, not a re-litigation.
#12 — --history 1 on both KV buckets currently means a suspected Decision #20 collision leaves no evidence to investigate; cheap, config-only fix.
Each states what's confirmed vs. what still needs deciding (a backoff schedule, an idempotency-key source, a TTL value, a history depth) so none of them read as silently settled. Let me know if you want to pick one to scope into an actual Decision/Track next, or leave them parked here for now.

